Gas Flow Control - Installation Guide
Project: Gas Flow Control
Release: v1.2
Prepared by: Andrew Sharp
Platform: Flutter Web App + Raspberry Pi 4 Controller 
1. Purpose
This guide explains how to access, install, and launch the Gas Flow Control application for review, demonstration, and future controller integration.
The application currently supports:
•	Secure sign-in
•	Six controller/tool positions
•	Valve-position controls
•	Individual valve close
•	Close All Valves
•	System status monitoring
•	Command history
•	Offline Demo Mode
•	Raspberry Pi controller communication
•	iPhone Home Screen installation
2. Web Application
The live application is available at:
https://gas-flow-control-demo.web.app
The web version can be opened from any modern browser.
For iPhone use, Safari is recommended.
3. Install on iPhone Home Screen
Initial Installation
1.	Connect the iPhone to the Internet.
2.	Open Safari.
3.	Navigate to:
https://gas-flow-control-demo.web.app
4.	Allow the application to fully load.
5.	Sign in once.
6.	Open the Home, Controls, and History screens once.
7.	Tap the Safari Share button.
8.	Select Add to Home Screen.
9.	Confirm the name:
Gas Flow Control
10.	Tap Add.
A Gas Flow Control icon should now appear on the iPhone Home Screen.
4. Offline Demo Mode
After the app has been loaded and installed once while online, Demo Mode can run without Internet access.
Offline Test
1.	Launch the app once while connected to the Internet.
2.	Close the app.
3.	Enable Airplane Mode.
4.	Confirm Wi-Fi and cellular data are unavailable.
5.	Launch Gas Flow Control from the Home Screen icon.
6.	Sign in.
7.	Open:
o	Home
o	Controls
o	History
The application should remain functional in Demo Mode.
5. Sign-In
The application uses:
•	Harder employee email
•	6-digit PIN
The accepted email domain and PIN are configured during the Flutter build process.
Example build configuration:
--dart-define=ACCESS_PIN=123456
--dart-define=HARDER_EMAIL_DOMAIN=harder.com
For production deployment, the current local PIN system should eventually be replaced with a centralized authentication system.
6. Demo Mode
Demo Mode is used when no physical controller is connected.
Demo Mode allows the operator to test:
•	Login
•	Home screen
•	Tool controls
•	Valve-position changes
•	Individual valve close
•	Close All Valves
•	Status behavior
•	Command history
•	Offline operation
Demo Mode does not operate physical gas-control hardware.
7. Raspberry Pi Controller Mode
Future field operation is intended to use a local wireless connection between the app and the Raspberry Pi controller.
Current controller architecture:
Gas Flow Control App
→ Local Network
→ Raspberry Pi 4 Model B
→ Gas Controller API
→ Valve Hardware Interface
→ Physical Valve Hardware
Internet access is not required for local controller communication once the final local deployment configuration is complete.
8. Raspberry Pi Controller Information
Current Raspberry Pi hostname:
GasControlPi
Current controller API port:
9000
Controller service:
gas-controller.service
The service is configured to start automatically when the Raspberry Pi boots.
9. Verify Raspberry Pi Controller
Connect to the Raspberry Pi over SSH:
ssh harderocotillo@PI_IP_ADDRESS
Check controller status:
sudo systemctl status gas-controller
Expected result:
Loaded: loaded (... enabled ...)
Active: active (running)
Exit the status viewer with:
q
10. Controller Health Check
From a computer on the same local network:
Invoke-RestMethod http://PI_IP_ADDRESS:9000/health
Expected response should include:
ok          : True
controller  : GasControlPi
hardware    : simulated-valve
tool_count  : 6
11. Source Code
The project source is stored in GitHub.
Repository:
https://github.com/asharpharder/gas_flow_control
Primary development branch:
master
Before making changes:
git pull origin master
After approved changes:
git add .
git commit -m "Describe change"
git push origin master
12. Web Deployment
The web application is deployed using Firebase Hosting.
Typical deployment workflow:
cd gas_flow_control
git pull origin master
cd flutter_app
flutter clean
flutter pub get
flutter analyze
Build:
flutter build web --no-web-resources-cdn --dart-define=DEMO_MODE=true --dart-define=OPERATOR_ID=field-review --dart-define=ACCESS_PIN=YOUR_PIN --dart-define=HARDER_EMAIL_DOMAIN=YOUR_DOMAIN
Then deploy:
cd ..
firebase.cmd deploy --only hosting
13. Release Verification
Before considering a release complete, verify:
•	Correct email + PIN signs in
•	Incorrect email is rejected
•	Incorrect PIN is rejected
•	Home screen loads
•	Controls screen loads
•	History screen loads
•	Valve commands work in Demo Mode
•	Individual Close Valve works
•	Close All Valves works
•	Sign Out returns to login
•	Offline iPhone launch works
•	Text and icons remain visible offline
•	Raspberry Pi controller responds on the local network
•	Git branch is clean
Expected Git status:
nothing to commit, working tree clean
14. Important Limitation
The current software is functionally complete as a prototype platform.
Physical production deployment still requires:
•	Final valve selection
•	Flow sensor selection
•	Pressure sensor selection
•	Argon CFH requirements
•	Regulated PSI requirements
•	Electrical integration
•	Calibration
•	Controlled gas testing
•	Field validation
•	Safety review
The application should not be considered a fully validated production gas-control system until those physical testing steps are complete.

